     iinniitt first tries to create the file /eettcc/bboooottttiimmee; this
     doubles as a check that the root file system is writable.
     If root came up read-only, because it was not cleanly un-
     mounted, the ccrreeaatt() fails and iinniitt repairs the root file
     system automatically: it finds the device root is mounted on
     by matching the device numbers of the entries in /ddeevv
     against that of /, runs cchheecckk -ss on that device (the file
     system is quiescent, since nothing can be open for writing
     on a read-only root), and then promotes root to read/write
     with mmoouunntt -ww, which re-reads the repaired super block and
     refuses if the file system is still dirty.

     iinniitt records the boot in /uussrr/aaddmm/wwttmmpp, and installs hand-
     lers for SSIIGGHHUUPP and SSIIGGQQUUIITT. With root writable it goes
     straight to multi-user: it runs /eettcc/rrcc if it exists, to
     complete multi-user setup, then reads /eettcc/ttttyyss and starts
     ggeettttyy on every enabled line listed there.  There is no
     single-user shell on a normal boot.

